 grain size, and mechanical properties. Lower finishing temperatures are associated with improved toughness and notch toughness, but the temperature must be selected and maintained carefully to avoid rapid grain growth, partial recrystallization, or other undesirable microstructural changes. [Data: Entities (166, 118); Relationships (157, 94)]\\n\\nThe importance of this variable is reinforced by strip-mill processing, where finishing-temperature selection and the distribution of reductions across processing regions are restricted. [Data: Entities (890, 931); Relationships (1227, 1253)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Niobium provides strengthening but requires tightly managed thermal processing\",\"explanation\":\"Nb-bearing steel benefits from niobium precipitation hardening, and controlled rolling is combined with Nb-bearing steel to improve notch toughness while retaining this strength increase. The deformation response in the two-phase region is described as broadly similar to Nb-free steel, although recovery behavior differs. [Data: Entities (82, 80); Relationships (84, 1464)]\\n\\nBefore hot rolling, Nb(C, N) must be dissolved at high temperature to make full use of precipitation hardening. However, this treatment coarsens the austenite grain structure, creating a processing trade-off between niobium utilization and grain refinement. [Data: Entities (113, 103); Relationships (70)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Microstructure and phase transformation determine toughness outcomes\",\"explanation\":\"The community links rolling conditions and deformation history to transformation from austenite into ferrite, acicular ferrite, bainite, and related structures. Transformation after deformation in the austenite–ferrite region can produce mixed structures containing equiaxed grains and subgrains. [Data: Entities (46); Relationships (35)]\\n\\nConversely, conventional hot rolling of coarse austenite can produce a mixture of coarse ferrite and bainite, which deteriorates notch toughness. This establishes microstructural refinement and controlled transformation as central mechanisms behind the preferred rolling route. [Data: Entities (104); Relationships (72, 73)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Notch toughness is the principal performance objective\",\"explanation\":\"Notch toughness is identified as a critical property for shipbuilding and structural steel plates because it helps prevent brittle fracture under low-temperature or impact loading. Controlled low-temperature hot rolling, including controlled finishing of Nb steel, is described as a means of refining microstructure and improving resistance to crack initiation and propagation. [Data: Entities (28, 67); Relationships (20, 53)]\\n\\nThe relationship between improved notch toughness and reduced brittle-fracture risk is explicit in the data. Brittle-fracture behavior is also described as anisotropic, with an orientation factor represented by cos²θ. [Data: Entities (641, 642); Relationships (876, 854)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Normalizing and composition controls provide related toughness strategies\",\"explanation\":\"Normalizing is a conventional heat-treatment route used to refine steel microstructure and improve notch toughness. The data describes controlled rolling as an alternative to normalizing in some mills, while controlled rolling followed by normalizing can provide additional refinement and improved strength–toughness performance in certain steels. [Data: Entities (15, 50, 53); Relationships (21, 48, 1627)]\\n\\nOther measures associated with meeting notch-toughness requirements include aluminum killing and increasing the manganese-to-carbon content ratio. These approaches are presented as complementary processing or composition controls rather than as replacements for all controlled-rolling practices. [Data: Entities (16, 123); Relationships (22, 100)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Strip-mill operation emphasizes integrated process control\",\"explanation\":\"The strip-mill process route is defined as a controlled-rolling route in which holding time and temperature, reduction distribution across recrystallization and non-recrystallization regions, and finishing-temperature selection are restricted. This indicates that the desired properties depend on coordinated control of multiple variables rather than on finishing temperature alone. [Data: Entities (890, 888, 931); Relationships (1227, 1253)]\\n\\nThe data also connects the processing community to the broader materials-design relationship between alloy composition, microstructure, and resulting properties. Silicon levels of approximately 0.3–0.5% are associated with a favorable strength–ductility balance in normalized and controlled-rolled steel. [Data: Entities (1110, 1111, 1114, 896, 907); Relationships (1603, 1608, 1188, 1203)]\"}],\"rating\":7.0,\"rating_explanation\":\"The community has high technical and industrial importance because it governs the strength, toughness, and brittle-fracture resistance of structural and shipbuilding steels, although no direct evidence of incidents or broader societal harm is provided.\"}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1790706852, "model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": "default", "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1399, "prompt_tokens": 5686, "total_tokens": 7085, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 76, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 5683, "cached_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_tokens": 5683}}, "formatted_response": null, "obfuscation": "PKM4I0i4H10AK1", "content": "{\"title\":\"Controlled Low-Temperature Rolling and Nb-Bearing Steel\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on controlled low-temperature hot rol